  to reverse, and it's the seam notifications will attach to.

  [1mThe fork:[0m when someone changes a task, what do we [3mrecord[0m about that change?

  - [1m(A) Current state only[0m — the task row is updated in place; nothing remembers what it was before. Simplest thing that
    works for a task app. But when notifications arrive, there's no record of "what changed" to build a notification
    from, so you'd be retrofitting change capture into every write path — touching all the mutation code a second time.
    Cheap now, pays interest later.
  - [1m(B) Event log beside the task row[0m — every mutation updates the task [3mand[0m appends an immutable [38;5;153mTaskEvent[39m ([38;5;153mtask_id[39m,
    [38;5;153mactor[39m, [38;5;153mtype[39m, [38;5;153mfrom[39m, [38;5;153mto[39m, [38;5;153mat[39m). Reads stay trivial (just query the task row); the event log is a pure addition.
    Notifications then become a [3mreader[0m of that log rather than a rewrite of the write path. Also gives you an activity
    feed and an audit trail for free.
  - [1m(C) Full event sourcing[0m — no mutable task row at all; state is folded from events. Conceptually cleanest, genuinely
    wrong for this. Every read either folds a history or maintains a projection you now have to keep correct. That's
    real complexity for a single-workspace team task list.

  [1mMy recommendation: (B).[0m It's the one choice in sub-project one that's made specifically because we know sub-project
  two is coming — it costs maybe a day now and converts notifications from "rewrite the write path" into "add a reader."
  I want to flag that honestly rather than smuggle it in: this is me spending a little of sub-project one's budget on
  sub-project two's behalf. If you'd rather keep sub-project one minimal and accept the retrofit, (A) is a legitimate
  call and I'll design it that way.
